WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.179
Welcome to Module 6, Cleaning and
Standardizing. In this

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:05.280
lesson you will learn how to identify

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.115
the observation unit of each GGIR output,
interpret processing

00:00:09.115 --> 00:00:11.280
information recorded in file names,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.045
apply approved cleaning rules, construct
cumulative bout

00:00:14.045 --> 00:00:15.280
measures, and standardize

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.421
variables while preserving traceability.
We will connect these

00:00:18.421 --> 00:00:19.840
concepts to practice through

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.548
a short Stata demonstration. Module 5 was
about checking data

00:00:24.548 --> 00:00:26.400
quality. We reviewed the

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.256
quality control outputs, inspected plots
and reports, and

00:00:29.256 --> 00:00:30.960
documented which records should be

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.962
kept, reviewed, or excluded. Module 6
starts only after those

00:00:34.962 --> 00:00:37.520
decisions are documented. Here we apply

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.937
the approved rules to the GGIR outputs,
clean night and

00:00:40.937 --> 00:00:43.360
day-level records, standardize variable

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.708
names and labels, and preserve a traceable
record of every

00:00:46.708 --> 00:00:48.960
transformation. We are not yet building

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.062
the final analysis dataset, combining
source files,

00:00:52.062 --> 00:00:54.800
calculating final participant-level
outcomes,

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.630
and creating the analysis-ready Stata
dataset come in Module

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.800
7. This boundary matters. Module 5

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.340
decides, Module 6 cleans and standardizes,
and Module 7

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.400
integrates the approved outputs.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.064
Before cleaning or merging any GGIR table,
always ask one

00:01:12.064 --> 00:01:14.400
question. What does one row represent?

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.768
In part2_summary, one row represents one
participant or

00:01:17.768 --> 00:01:19.360
recording file. Therefore,

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.514
ID alone should uniquely identify each
row. In

00:01:22.514 --> 00:01:25.120
part2_daysummary, the same participant

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.890
can appear on several calendar dates.
Here, one row represents

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.200
one participant-day and the unique key

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.880
is ID plus calendar date. In
part4_nightsummary, one

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.720
participant can contribute

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.711
several nights. One row therefore
represents one

00:01:39.711 --> 00:01:42.640
participant-night and the key is ID plus
night.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.427
Finally, part5_personsummary is
participant-level. Each row

00:01:46.427 --> 00:01:48.160
represents one participant,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.447
so ID should again be unique. These
observation units

00:01:51.447 --> 00:01:53.680
determine which cleaning and merging

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.731
operations are appropriate. Checking the
key first prevents

00:01:56.731 --> 00:01:58.800
duplicated rows, silent inflation of the

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.764
dataset, and incorrect summaries later in
the workflow. GGIR

00:02:03.764 --> 00:02:06.080
file names contain important

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.924
information about how the data were
processed. The

00:02:08.924 --> 00:02:11.200
part5_personsummary prefix tells us that

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.681
this is a participant-level output created
in Part 5. The

00:02:15.681 --> 00:02:17.920
letters WW mean wake to wake.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.460
This identifies the definition of a day
used for this output.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.480
L40, M100, and V400 record the light,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.866
moderate, and vigorous acceleration
thresholds. T5 records the

00:02:30.866 --> 00:02:33.200
time threshold and A5 records the

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.811
angle threshold. Together, these blocks
identify the

00:02:35.811 --> 00:02:37.920
processing settings that produce the file.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.352
Our project uses L40, M100, V400, T5, and
A5. Do not replace

00:02:43.352 --> 00:02:46.240
these values with thresholds from a

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.533
generic example. If the settings change,
the file name and

00:02:49.533 --> 00:02:51.520
resulting measures may no longer be

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.197
directly comparable. Reading the file name
before importing

00:02:55.197 --> 00:02:57.440
the data helps us select the correct

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.722
source file and prevents us from combining
outputs created

00:03:00.722 --> 00:03:02.080
with different settings.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.982
Before applying an exclusion, inspect
cleaningcode. In this

00:03:06.982 --> 00:03:08.720
GGIR 3.3.6 course run,

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.087
sleep_full contains 40 participant-night
records: 33 have

00:03:13.087 --> 00:03:14.160
cleaningcode 1

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.833
and 7 have cleaningcode 2. With no sleep
log, the project

00:03:18.833 --> 00:03:20.800
codebook says to exclude

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.766
codes 2 through 6 for this demonstration;
all 7 exclusions

00:03:26.766 --> 00:03:28.000
have code 2.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.831
This is course-specific, not a universal
GGIR rule. Always

00:03:31.831 --> 00:03:33.680
preserve the original source

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.411
and save the cleaned records as a new
traceable file. Now

00:03:37.411 --> 00:03:39.440
let's apply this rule in Stata.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.041
This recording demonstrates the verified
module 6 workflow in

00:03:45.041 --> 00:03:46.080
a series of short,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.882
auditable steps. We begin with the full
part 4 night summary.

00:03:49.882 --> 00:03:51.440
After importing the file,

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.541
we confirm the required variables and
verify that participant

00:03:55.541 --> 00:03:57.760
ID plus night uniquely identifies

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.619
every row. Before applying any exclusion,
we examine the

00:04:02.619 --> 00:04:03.920
distribution of

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.947
cleaningcode. This GGIR 3.3.6 sleep_full
course run has 40

00:04:08.947 --> 00:04:10.160
night records;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.623
7 have cleaningcode 2. With no sleep log
and after confirming

00:04:14.623 --> 00:04:16.160
the project codebook,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.561
the approved course rule removes codes 2
through 6. We then

00:04:20.561 --> 00:04:21.680
verify that the

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:25.127
number of records before cleaning equals
the number removed

00:04:25.127 --> 00:04:26.880
plus the number remaining. The

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.691
cleaned night-level data are saved as a
new file while the

00:04:30.691 --> 00:04:33.200
original GGIR source remains unchanged.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.501
Next, we import the part 5 day summary.
One additional

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:38.640
participant-day has cleaning code 2

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.116
and is removed, leaving 31 participant-day
records. The code

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:45.280
then combines GGIR's mutually

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.983
exclusive bout intervals into cumulative
measures. These

00:04:48.983 --> 00:04:51.760
include MVPA lasting at least 1, 5, and 10

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:55.382
minutes as well as inactivity lasting at
least 20 minutes. We

00:04:55.382 --> 00:04:57.520
verify the expected nesting of these

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.435
measures. For example, the 1 minute MVPA
measure cannot be

00:05:01.435 --> 00:05:04.000
smaller than the 5 minute or 10 minute

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.028
measure. Finally, the participant
identifier, calendar date,

00:05:08.028 --> 00:05:09.840
variable names, labels, and

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.722
measurement units are standardized. The
workflow checks unique

00:05:13.722 --> 00:05:15.600
keys and missing values, saves

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.896
cleaned Stata and CSV files, and creates
documentation for

00:05:19.896 --> 00:05:21.600
traceability. The final

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.203
screen shows the cleaned participant-day
dataset. Module 7

00:05:25.203 --> 00:05:27.440
will use these documented outputs to

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.653
construct the final analysis dataset. GGIR
reports bout

00:05:31.653 --> 00:05:34.640
duration using mutually exclusive bins.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.118
To create cumulative measures, we combine
these existing bins

00:05:38.118 --> 00:05:40.000
in Stata. MVPA lasting at least 1

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.715
minute is the sum of the 1 to less than 5,
5 to less than 10,

00:05:43.715 --> 00:05:45.360
and 10 or more minute bins.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.371
MVPA lasting at least 5 minutes combines
the last 2 bins,

00:05:49.371 --> 00:05:51.440
while MVPA lasting at least 10

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.918
minutes uses the 10 or more minute bin
directly. Inactivity

00:05:54.918 --> 00:05:57.040
lasting at least 20 minutes combines

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.012
the 20 to less than 30 and 30 or more
minute bins. These

00:06:01.012 --> 00:06:03.920
outcomes represent cumulative minutes per

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:08.465
participant-day. Stata combines the GGIR
outputs. It does not

00:06:08.465 --> 00:06:11.520
recreate bouts from the raw accelerometer

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.434
signal. Finally, standardize the variable
names while

00:06:15.434 --> 00:06:18.240
preserving their original meaning. For

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.755
example, ID becomes participant_id,
calendar_date becomes

00:06:21.755 --> 00:06:24.160
activity_date, and cleaningcode becomes

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.134
sleep_clean_code. The long GGIR bout
variable

00:06:27.134 --> 00:06:31.033
dur_day_MVPA_bts_10_min is renamed
mvpa_bout10_min. We then

00:06:31.033 --> 00:06:31.760
verify that

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.419
participant ID and activity date form a
unique key. The

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:38.080
standardized dataset is exported in both

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.004
Stata and CSV formats. A data dictionary
records the original

00:06:42.004 --> 00:06:44.320
and standardized names, source file,

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:47.467
observation unit, measurement unit,
transformation, missing

00:06:47.467 --> 00:06:49.440
data rule, and retention or exclusion

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.961
decision. This creates a clear and
reproducible record of

00:06:52.961 --> 00:06:54.320
every processing step.
